// @check
// @error `==` is not overloadable — every type compares structurally, and a `function "=="` declaration would be ignored
// @error `==` is not overloadable — every type compares structurally, and a `function "=="` declaration would be ignored
// @error `!=` is not overloadable — every type compares structurally, and a `function "!="` declaration would be ignored
// @error `!=` is not overloadable — every type compares structurally, and a `function "!="` declaration would be ignored
// GRADUATED from `silent-class-inventory` D46, the one row in that file whose filed outcome was
// a check-clean SILENTLY WRONG VALUE rather than an invalid module — the class it ranks above
// invalid wasm, because nothing refuses it.
//
// A `function "=="` declaration PARSED, TYPE-CHECKED AND DID NOTHING. `checkBinary` returns on
// the equality arm before the operator-dispatch tail and `drwDispatchOp` excludes `==`/`!=` at
// the emitter, so the structural compare ran instead and the program printed the answer the
// declaration was written to change. The only diagnostics were `Unused parameter self/other` —
// the witness's own, and a CONSEQUENCE of needing a body that disagrees with the fallback to be
// observable at all. Nothing said the declaration was inert.
//
// AND THE COMPILER ASKED FOR IT BY NAME. `eqRefusals`' first sentence used to end
// `— define a `==` operator for it`, so a reader refused a `K[]` comparison was instructed to
// write exactly this. Doing it changed nothing: the same two errors came back.
//
// REJECTED RATHER THAN IMPLEMENTED, and the deciding measurement is who the diagnostic's
// customer is. It fires on a CONTAINER — `K[]`, `Circle[]` — whose compare recurses through
// `emitStructEqRec`, `emitListEqRCore` and `emitListEqSCore`, three cores with no per-element
// dispatch hook, and through `isEquatable`, std's four `needle: T` exports and the map key.
// Honouring the top-level struct case alone would leave the message still prescribing something
// inert one container deep, which MOVES the trap rather than removing it. Nothing in the tree
// declares one, so the reject costs no capability, and the remedy clause was retired in the
// same change — a diagnostic recommending a declaration the compiler now refuses would be
// worse than one recommending an ignored one.
//
// FOUR SPELLINGS, ONE HOME. The symbol-token name (`function ==(…)`) and the quoted name
// (`function "=="(…)`) converge on the same `name` in `parseFuncHead`, so one test covers both,
// and `!=` is the same rule — it was silently ignored too, and its witness printed `true` where
// the declaration said `false`.
type Circle = { r: i32 }

function "=="(self: Circle, other: Circle): boolean { true }

function ==(self: Circle, other: Circle): boolean { true }

function "!="(self: Circle, other: Circle): boolean { false }

function !=(self: Circle, other: Circle): boolean { false }

function cell(a: Circle, b: Circle): boolean { a == b }

print(cell({ r: 1 }, { r: 2 }))
